# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
plato-gorgias-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2 has "¬rhet_most_skilled → O ¬rhet_counsel" and "¬rhet_advises_building" and "¬rhet_advises_war"; plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.2 and plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.3 have "O(rhet_counsel → rhet_most_skilled)" and "¬rhet_advises_building" and "¬rhet_advises_war"; they differ when only "the rhetorician is the one most skilled in the craft for which the election is held" is true, which the first allows
plato-gorgias-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.2 and plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the rhetorician is the one most skilled in the craft for which the election is held" is true and "the rhetorician, not the master workman, will advise when walls, harbours or docks have to be built", "the rhetoricians, not the military, will advise when generals have to be chosen, an order of battle arranged or a position taken", "it ought to be that (if the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman, then the rhetorician is the one most skilled in the craft for which the election is held)", "it ought to be that (if the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman, then the rhetorician is the one most skilled in the craft for which the election is held)" and "it ought to be that it is not so that the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman" are false. Is S4, "For at every election he ought to be chosen who is most skilled; and, again, when walls have to be built or harbours or docks to be constructed, not the rhetorician but the master workman will advise; or when generals have to be chosen and an order of battle arranged, or a position taken, then the military will advise and not the rhetoricians: what do you say, Gorgias?", true then?
plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2 has "¬rhet_most_skilled" and "O ¬rhet_counsel → ¬rhet_counsel"; plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.2 has "¬rhet_most_skilled"; they differ when only "the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman" and "it ought to be that it is not so that the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman" are true, which the second allows; plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.3 has "¬rhet_most_skilled" and "O(rhet_counsel → rhet_most_skilled) → (rhet_counsel → rhet_most_skilled)"
plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (it ought to be that it is not so that the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman), then it is not so that the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman"; plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.2 does not
plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (it ought to be that it is not so that the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman), then it is not so that the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (it ought to be that (if the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman, then the rhetorician is the one most skilled in the craft for which the election is held)), then (if the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman, then the rhetorician is the one most skilled in the craft for which the election is held)"; plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.2 does not
plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2 and plato-gorgias-1.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (it ought to be that (if the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman, then the rhetorician is the one most skilled in the craft for which the election is held)), then (if the rhetorician will be taken into counsel when the assembly meets to elect a physician, a shipwright or any other craftsman, then the rhetorician is the one most skilled in the craft for which the election is held)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
